--- !u!1001 &5027988053151077508
PrefabInstance:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  serializedVersion: 2
  m_Modification:
    serializedVersion: 3
    m_TransformParent: {fileID: 2847687633723015916}
    m_Modifications:
    - target: {fileID: 695126119417923021, guid: ea594af9f1312f74db4ddc844a19a09a, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_Layer
      value: 6
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 2810564045043720407, guid: ea594af9f1312f74db4ddc844a19a09a, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_Layer
      value: 6
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5277765510278748903, guid: ea594af9f1312f74db4ddc844a19a09a, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_Layer
      value: 6
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 7250336164257311119, guid: ea594af9f1312f74db4ddc844a19a09a, type: 3}
      propertyPath: Scrolling
      value: 1
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 7250336164257311119, guid: ea594af9f1312f74db4ddc844a19a09a, type: 3}
      propertyPath: TextContent
      value: "\tI enjoy Survival Horror games and wanted to try to make one.\r\n\tThis
        is from a Unity project I was working on a couple months back\r\n\tIt was
        built for a sense of creeping horror, with a scary backstory discovered bit
        by bit.\r\n \tSomething like Amnesia with gameplay elements of Skyrim.\r\n\t\r\n\tSo
        I began creating a collection of objects that fit the story I had\r\n\tLike
        these tablets.\r\n\tThese were two tests, one a basic tablet, and the other
        a more refined design.\r\n\tI wanted weird ancient tablets that no one could
        translate.\r\n\tOr that they shouldn't be translating.  Definitely not reading
        aloud.\r\n\t\r\n\tI love diagetic text, and I thought of making books that
        could be physically opened and read.\r\n\tI made a generic book that used
        a separate texture layer to swap out gilded covers and bindings, \r\n\tAllowing
        many books that would have their own covers.\r\n\tHere's one of them.  This
        is an actual centuries-old book on alchemy.\r\n\tI have other texts on hand
        with more content, but this one was the initial test.\r\n\t\r\n\tI used low
        ambiant lighting with short-range point lights get high contrast between
        light and shadow.\r\n\tI wanted what light there was to look lonely, islands
        of light in the darkness.\r\n\tThe player could pick up and carry a light
        with them, and I wanted them to need it.\r\n\tIt would be enough to illuminate
        what they were looking at, but not much else.\r\n\tDarkness is a good way
        to make environments look better, just like in movies.\r\n\tI needed furniture
        to fill out rooms - tables, shelves, desks.  Room clutter.\r\n\tAll of the
        individual shelves were given colliders so they can hold objects.\r\n\t\r\n\tPortable
        lights could be used to get a better look at details on objects.\r\n\tMoving
        it in close to get a good look at what's there.\r\n\r\n\tI built doors to
        open and close, manually or by impact.\r\n\tThe idea was a small enemy couldn't
        get through, but a large one could knock it open.\r\n\r\n\tI added more rooms,
        like this long hallway.\r\n\tI threw in some more visual details, like stone
        columns, but I really wasn't sure what Look I was going for yet.\r\n\tI tried
        to make a working mirror using camera tricks.  Mirrors are hard.\r\n\tAs
        you can see the player didn't have a model yet.\r\n\t\r\n\tAnd THAT is one
        of the enemies.\r\n\tI used Unity's AI navigation system to help him move
        from room to room.\r\n\tThe idea was to use posessed objects as hostile attackers.\r\n\tPartly
        because I couldn't build a humanoid one that would match the realism I was
        going for.\r\n\tAnd partly because I realized how scary it would be to not
        be able to identify what's hostile if it's not moving.\r\n\tThese enemies
        are made to easily hide right in front of you.\r\n\tIf you come into a room
        and see four chairs, an observant player might notice a fifth one on a return
        trip.\r\n\tIf it's sitting still, it's just looks like a chair.\r\n\r\n\tI
        always thought the alchemy elements of both Amnesia and Skyrim were interesting.\r\n\tIt's
        not something you see often in games, a transitional science that died as
        we learned how the world really worked.\r\n\tBut there's a lot of potential
        in it, gameplay-wise.\r\n\tSkyrim used it to add more fantasy flavor to its
        world.\r\n\tAmnesia used it to amp up the horror with hints of forbidden
        knowledge.\r\n\tSo I worked on it.  But I wasn't satisfied with what I've
        seen in other games.\r\n\tI wanted bottles that held variable amounts of
        liquid.\r\n\tI wanted to transfer materials from container to container.\r\n\tI
        wanted the player to do the work.  I wanted room for accidents.\r\n\r\n\tI
        got it by creating two concentric bottle models, one glass and one liquid.\r\n\tThe
        liquid is governed by a shader to do a cutaway along the horizontal plane.\r\n\tI
        gave it a particle emitter for drops of fluid\r\n\tI also had it creating
        puddles when it lands on a surface.\r\n\tBottles have a bubble effect to
        simulate boiling, \r\n\tAnd I was working on fluid transfer from container
        to container.\r\n\tThis was just a prototype, my first stab at it, but it
        was a good start.\r\n\r\n\tI added alchemical gear the player would need,
        along with other assorted clutter.\r\n\tEverything is made to be picked up
        and moved, if it's not too heavy.\r\n\tEverything is interactable.\r\n\t\t\t\t\r\n\tMaking
        this was fun and educational, but I realized it was really a multi-person
        project\r\n\tI'm a programmer, not a level developer or graphic artist\r\n\tThe
        sheer amount of objects and layouts needed was high, too high for me alone.\r\n\tSo
        I moved on to other more doable projects.\r\n\tLooking back at this code
        I must have grown since I wrote this.\r\n\tBecause all the technical problems
        I had have simple solutions that I can see now.\r\n\tI'd love to come back
        to this, but I would need more people.Alchemy Game Demo:\r\n"
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
